export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>A couple of weeks ago, the developer released a new cinematic introducing their newest addition to the champion roster. Yone, Yasuo's deceased brother, somehow returned to the land of Ionia in pursuit of eradicating demonic presence. The two brothers share a lot in common, but there are obviously a couple distinct differences that set them apart, namely the colors. While Yasuo's attire is full of blue, Yone's design is highlighted with Red. Since this motif is very common in pop-culture, we think it is finally time to talk about the Red Oni, Blue Oni trope.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/2319752b20f045018e9b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/2319752b20f045018e9b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:305px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/86ef073a0b164044eb2a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/86ef073a0b164044eb2a.webp\" width=\"886\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:261px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>What is this trope, anyway?</strong></p>\n<p>Red Oni, Blue Oni is a common design motif in Japanese animations and games. The basic idea is to create one character embodying the color red, the other blue. The red character is generally aggressive and energetic; the blue character, on the other hand, is more introspective and level-headed. They are the two ends of a spectrum. Therefore, the use of red and blue not only adds to visual interest, but also the personality and depth of these characters.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>This trope also bleeds into western character design. Ultimately, it boils down to using red for hot-headed characters and blue for calm, cool characters.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/c5eafe347f89a9a163b3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/c5eafe347f89a9a163b3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"667\" style=\"--source-width:305px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/01f5c9cbe8ce65d2007b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/01f5c9cbe8ce65d2007b.webp\" width=\"813\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:261px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Videogame examples</strong></p>\n<p>Within the game territory, we needn't look further than the developer's portfolio to understand how Japanese designers utilize this trope. Their mascots are the embodiments of this red versus blue mentality. In <em>Street Fighter,</em> we have Ryu and Ken; the former's signature move is Hadouken, which hurls a blue fire ball, and the latter's is Shoryuken, which is a red flaming uppercut. Being the blue guy, Ryu requires players to be calm and keep their distance with Hadouken; they should only strike at opportune moments. On the other hand, Ken excels at rushing down his opponents with consecutive melee pressure.</p>\n<p>The next example comes from the <em>Mega Man X</em> series, in which we have the blue X and the red Zero. In terms of gameplay, they are quite similar to Ryu and Ken: X specializes in shooting mechanics while Zero is more effective at close range. In the story, Zero was originally built to destroy X, but he turned out to be an ally. However, there is still corruption in his systems, which make him go berserk sometimes.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>But perhaps, there is no better illustration for Red Oni, Blue Oni than the sibling rivalry in <em>Devil May Cry 3</em>. The red-coated young brother Dante is a playful, witty teenage boy whose main concern in fighting is being stylish. His brother Vergil, in contrast, dons himself with a blue trench coat and always acts cold; his approach to combat is precise and quick, striking enemies down with as few movements as possible. And between them is Lady, a girl with red and blue heterochromia. Without diving to deep into the story, she is stuck within the conflicts of the two brothers, and later becomes the catalyst for Dante to stop Vergil.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/b394dd06fa421355be6c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/b394dd06fa421355be6c.webp\" width=\"734\" height=\"551\" style=\"--source-width:305px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/381f19e3e83cc301c7c0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/381f19e3e83cc301c7c0.webp\" width=\"784\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:261px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Kin of the Stained Blade</strong></p>\n<p>Now that we have understood this red and blue trope, we can easily see the visual dynamics between Yasuo and Yone. These two brothers grew up together and practiced the same school of martial art. Despite differences in fighting styles, there is still a spiritual bond between them. This bond is displayed to us via the blue attire that they wear at the beginning of the cinematic. However, Yone had a red band around his waist, which attaches to his swords; Yasuo later picked it up and tied it around his sheath in memory of his brother. So, there is still a shed of red in Yasuo's blue exterior.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Toward the last half of the cinematic when the two brothers fight together, we also see some light sheds of blue in Yone—in his spirit eyes and his mortal blade. Although the mask and the demon sword are his most pronounced features, his human characteristics still remain as an indicator that he is still connected to Yasuo in some way.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/94da240921367234410f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/94da240921367234410f.webp\" width=\"1118\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:261px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This particular moment showcases the red and blue interplay very clearly. The people behind this cinematic might have taken some inspiration from Devil May Cry 5's ending.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/f41dcaf5abc44a278f3f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/f41dcaf5abc44a278f3f.webp\" width=\"836\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:305px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>The key is contrast</strong></p>\n<p>\"If you only took one thing from design school, I think it would be the word <em>contrast</em>.\"—Pete Docter. The Red Oni, Blue Oni trope wasn't invented because someone randomly picked two different colors; people were looking for something that could oppose and complement one another, visually and narratively. You don't even have to stick to red and blue exclusively; black and white, dark and light, or different shapes and sizes, there are plenty of ways to create contrast. As long as the designs reinforce the storytelling, you are free to experiment.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
